INT. DRIVE-IN CLIMBING WALL - NIGHT

The back of a derelict drive-in screen, twelve stories of cracked white plywood, now bolted with colored holds and lit by a single sodium lamp. Weeds push through the empty parking lot below. A thermos steams on a folding table at the base.

PERPETUA "PEP" ILUNGA, 24, wiry, chalk to the elbows, hangs from a sloping overhang thirty feet up. Her forearms are knotted. She breathes through her teeth, reaching for a hold the size of a bottle cap and missing it by an inch. She swings out, catches herself, and swears softly to the dark.

Her fingers slide off. She falls, and the rope catches her with a soft whump. She hangs spinning in her harness, the lot turning below her, then reaches out and grabs the wall again. She lets out a laugh that is half sob, half triumph.

PEP
Again.

She climbs. Hand over hand, foot to foot, the slow rhythm of someone who has done this route two hundred times. She reaches the overhang and sticks the bottle cap this time. Her whole body locks. She hangs there, shaking, victorious, and for a breath she is perfectly still.

Then a car engine on gravel. Headlights sweep the wall and pin her, a bug on a screen. She twists her head. The car, a dented sedan, idles at the edge of the lot.

A door opens. BARNABY CROSS, 50s, thin, in a windbreaker and a ball cap, steps out holding a clipboard. He does not look up. He walks to the folding table, picks up the thermos, sniffs it, and sets it down.

BARNABY
(calling up)
Perpetua Ilunga?

PEP
Who's asking?

BARNABY
County. Code enforcement.

The word lands like a stone in a pond. Her grip loosens. She slips a foot, catches it, and clings on with new fury.

PEP
It's private property.

BARNABY
It's a structure over fifty feet without a permit.

PEP
It's a screen. It's been here since 1962.

BARNABY
That's the problem.

She presses her forehead against the plywood. Below, Barnaby writes something on the clipboard. Then, to her astonishment, he sets it on the table, takes off his ball cap, and rolls his shoulders.

BARNABY (CONT'D)
Is that a V6?

PEP
What?

BARNABY
The problem you're on. The one with the sloper.

PEP
(slowly)
Yes?

BARNABY
Left heel hook. Then you can pull through. You're fighting your own hips.

She stares at him. Silence. Then she shifts her left foot, hooks the heel on a hold, and her hips swing into the wall. The next move unlocks. She reaches the top, slaps the lip, and shouts.

PEP
It worked!

Barnaby nods. He picks up the clipboard, tears off a yellow slip, and tucks it under the thermos.

BARNABY
You have thirty days.

PEP
To do what?

BARNABY
Come see me. I've got a form.

She laughs, breathless, and lets go, falling the last of the way down the rope. Barnaby puts his cap back on, climbs into his car, and drives off. Pep dangles in the lamp light, turning, the yellow slip fluttering under the thermos.
